{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The mechanism conflates a causal process with a mere behavioral description. 'Chunking' is offered as the reason experts skip steps, but the target never specifies the causal chain by which non-experts applying a checklist converts that behavioral difference into a measurably higher rate of objectively correct outcomes. It asserts that capturing 'low-hanging' errors increases reliability, yet it provides no mechanism connecting the elimination of expert blind spots to net performance superiority over the expert on the full task distribution. The expert may skip trivial steps but compensate via domain knowledge; the target identifies a failure mode but does not causally demonstrate that its elimination yields higher average correctness rather than merely a different error profile.","counterexample":[{"task":"binary medical triage","expert":"skips a 'check pulse' step but correctly triages 95% of cases via pattern recognition","panel":"dutifully checks pulse on every case, catches 1 case the expert missed, but misclassifies 10 ambiguous cases the expert got right due to lack of knowledge","result":"panel is less reliable on average despite eliminating the named blind spot"}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"The variance-reduction mechanism is asserted, not causally derived. Labeling the panel a 'distributed processing unit' and invoking 'single point of failure' is metaphorical; it does not supply the causal mechanism by which distributing cognition across non-experts with a checklist shifts mean reliability above the expert. The target even concedes 'lower-peak' performance but never causally explains why the reduction in catastrophic-failure probability outweighs the loss of high-peak performance on tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, leaving the central reliability claim unsupported by a demonstrated causal chain.","counterexample":[{"task":"mathematical theorem verification","expert":"has 90% per-item accuracy with rare catastrophic failures","panel":"each non-expert has 55% accuracy; checklist reduces but does not eliminate systemic reasoning errors","result":"panel majority vote accuracy can fall below the expert's 90% because the checklist cannot inject domain competence the panel lacks, so variance reduction does not translate to mean superiority"}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":false,"case":"","counterexample":null}]}